when I was 10 years old my parents took  me and my little brother to Vegas for my  birthday when I was a kid I was really  into magic and there was this  world-famous magician by the name of  Lance Burton who had a show at the Monte  Carlo Resort Casino my parents had  managed to get tickets for the night of  my birthday the show was absolutely  incredible  it started off with a bang and with each  consecutive trick it kept getting bigger  and better more outlandish more  incredible and the audience loved it and  then it came time for the climax of the  show Lance Burton came rolling out in a  bright red Corvette that was packed full  of showgirls and when I say this car was  packed folks showgirls I mean there were  so many showgirls in and around this car  you could barely tell it was a Corvette  so that's Burton came rolling out parked  the car right in middle of stage he got  out of the driver's seat walked in front  of it he took this long slow look at the  audience  and they came back to me and we locked  eyes and I was absolutely terrified  maybe even a little bit more terrified  than am right now this was one of my  heroes one of my idols from the best  magicians in the entire world and over  the past hour I had seen him do some of  the craziest tricks I've ever seen to be  honest with you at that point I wanted  nothing to do with him but he began to  tell me this story story about how when  he was my age his one wish his biggest  dream was to take a ride in a red  Corvette filled with showgirls and then  he asked me if that's what I wanted to  do tonight absolutely terrified  I remember I was gripping the sides of  my chair so tight I had white knuckles  and my jaw felt like it was clenched  shut after what felt like forever I  finally managed to eke out a very simple  answer his question remember going yeah  sure so he invites me up on the stage  and I remember I was bit huskier back  then but I got on my chair and I ran  over the sides they  quickest like been there was a showgirl  arm stretched out she grabs my hand she  brings him to Lance Lance puts me right  in the driver's seat of this bright red  Corvette all the showgirls piled back in  and the curtains go up and all of a  sudden lights go off and just as quick  as it had started it was all over the  lights came back on except this time I  was backstage and I remember looking out  from the car and seeing what seemed to  be miles and miles of props gadgets and  gizmos at lantry's than his show it was  a really incredible sight but something  that I know I'll never forget were these  television monitors that they had set up  all around the backstage that showed  what was going on up in front of the  stage from the audience's point of view  and we had gotten back there with just  enough time to see the reveal to the  audience that me and the showgirls in  the car had disappeared when that  curtain fell the audience went  absolutely crazy they loved it and I'll  never forget it because it was a really  strange feeling for me it was a strange  feeling of disconnectedness between what  I had just experienced and the story  that Lance was telling the audience and  this always stands out in my mind as an  excellent example of the power of  storytelling because the audience knew  that I didn't go on this magical car ID  and this Corvette filled with showgirls  I knew that it didn't happen that it  couldn't happen  but the story that Lance was telling and  the way in which he was doing it was so  powerful that they wanted to believe it  so it always stands out as that as a  perfect example of power storytelling  the ability to capture an audience's  attention motivate them and call them to  action now no person knows about the  true power of storytelling more than man  by the name of Peter Guber now Peter  Guber is a Hollywood executive a UCLA  professor and the chairman of Mandalay  Entertainment Group I came across a book  he had written called tell to win back  when I was an undergrad  and this book is entirely about the  power of storytelling in business and  I'll never forget the story the Peter  Guber uses to introduce his book it's a  perfect example of the critical  difference between data dumps and  purposeful stories so to tell that story  let's go back to Vegas except this time  instead of me it's Peter Guber and he's  sitting in the then mayor of Las Vegas  his office man by the name of mayor  Oscar Goodman and he's there to convince  the mayor that they should invest in a  brand-new state-of-the-art ballpark for  the minor league baseball team that man  will entertain me Group owned Peter  Guber remembers looking at each wall of  the office and recalling seeing  countless pieces of Hollywood  memorabilia what seemed to be an  unlimited amount of pictures of the  mayor with practically every star in  Hollywood everything about this guy  screamed major league when it came time  to start the presentation Peter Guber  began rattling off all the facts and  figures he had collected data point  after data point metric after metric all  the information about how mainly  entertainment was going to boost  revenues and cut costs and in the middle  of his one of his presentation the mayor  stopped him Denis trout's  he asked Peter Guber a very simple  question yeah soon can you bring a major  league baseball team to Las Vegas  now Peter Guber was a bit stunned at  this point he wasn't talking about  bringing the major league baseball team  in Las Vegas he was talking about  building a minor league ballpark so he  tried to rehash the same facts and  figures that he used before to try to  convince some errors but unfortunately  was unsuccessful the mayor never saw his  point eventually Manley entertainment  ended up selling the baseball team Peter  Guber looked back on this experience  later and tried to figure out what had  gone wrong then he realized that he'd  committed a fatal flaw even though he  was in the show business he had  forgotten to tell the mayor story when  he forgot to tell the mayor story he  lost some areas interest and we lost  mayor's interest he didn't have any  ability to motivate him the mayor never  saw the point that Peter Guber was  trying to make now this highlights the  crucial  difference between non stories and  purposeful stories you see non stories  like PowerPoint presentations filled  with all the facts and figures sure they  can convey information and sometimes  that's great  but there's nothing like a purposeful  story to move the hearts minds feet and  wallets of an audience in the speaker's  intended direction now luckily for us it  doesn't take much to tell a good story  or at least a decent one and only takes  three things and it's the same three  things that we've been using tell  stories since the beginning of humankind  and I'll give them to you tonight all we  need is one a challenge - a struggle to  overcome that challenge and three a  resolution that's it challenge struggle  resolution now let's go back to the  first story I told the one about me  disappearing on stage with Lance Berkman  I'm actually missing one of those I'm  missing the resolution some of you may  be disappointed find out that I did not  live out the rest of my life as a prop  in Lance Burton show did make it back to  see my family but we had to use my  reveal back to the audience as the  resolution his show much like I'm using  him as a resolution to my show tonight  the moment was just right Lance revealed  me and him sitting right in the middle  of the audience with a spotlight shining  brightly down on us and the audience  realized that we were there they  absolutely loved it Lance wasn't  finished quite yet he invited he invited  me back up on stage to give me a parting  gift can you so this was a shirt of me  and Lance Burton or it's picture of me  my turn with the shirt that I have even  to this day it's shirt that reminds me  to tell good stories sure the ones that  I love and encourage others around me to  do the same thank you very much
 

